emy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "897ac2ce2f818b4074ca75574a898c603a723d3183bee0d3077541e6d240c024" "basis" 1452 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "897ac2ce2f818b4074ca75574a898c603a723d3183bee0d3077541e6d240c024" "basis" 1452 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "897ac2ce2f818b4074ca75574a898c603a723d3183bee0d3077541e6d240c024" "basis" 1452 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "897ac2ce2f818b4074ca75574a898c603a723d3183bee0d3077541e6d240c024" "basis" 1452 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-332266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12x-13.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -12(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-13\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha x+(\\alpha -12\\beta )=12x-13.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -12\\beta =-13\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f84f07fec1207335110caad444df3ccbacc046c8014135dad68ca0b6d139120"} {"question" "q-332266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332266" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f84f07fec1207335110caad444df3ccbacc046c8014135dad68ca0b6d139120"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}-5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f84f07fec1207335110caad444df3ccbacc046c8014135dad68ca0b6d139120"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{-6x}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f84f07fec1207335110caad444df3ccbacc046c8014135dad68ca0b6d139120"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-6\\beta =12\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =0 \\\\ A-2 & =0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\,{e}^{6x}-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3f84f07fec1207335110caad444df3ccbacc046c8014135dad68ca0b6d139120"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"} {"question" "q-332780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^{6x}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081054/state.json" "file_sha256" "469ad10b4c36121fc1e0f3134bd7e8e0404662578c4c15b377b789897a70d718"}] "usage" {"q-332302" [] "q-332780" [] "q-332552" [] "q-332266" [] "q-332547" []} "basis" 2841 "retractions" [[17592186229578 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y = 12x - 13$.\n\nFind the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186229578 :question/worked-solution "Try $y_p=ax+b$, so $y_p^{\\prime}=a$. Substitution gives $a-12(ax+b)=12x-13$. Comparing coefficients, $-12a=12$ and $a-12b=-13$. Thus $a=-1$, $b=1$, and $y_p(x)=1-x$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186238171 17592186301229 17592186301230 17592186301275 17592186302275 17592186308633 17592186308634 17592186308635 17592186308636 17592186308637] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228011 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228011 :answer-field/id] [17592186228011 :answer-field/key] [17592186228011 :answer-field/type] [17592186301228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186301228 :answer-field/id] [17592186301228 :answer-field/key] [17592186301228 :answer-field/type] [17592186301274 :answer-field/correct] [17592186301274 :answer-field/id] [17592186301274 :answer-field/key] [17592186301274 :answer-field/type] [17592186302274 :answer-field/correct] [17592186302274 :answer-field/id] [17592186302274 :answer-field/key] [17592186302274 :answer-field/type] [17592186308632 :answer-field/correct] [17592186308632 :answer-field/id] [17592186308632 :answer-field/key] [17592186308632 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
